# .cursorrules — Neural-Rip v4.0 / Origami Protocol
# Applied to every AI agent session in this workspace.

## Non-Negotiable Priorities

### 1. Apple Silicon ANE-First
- All CoreML inference MUST target `MLComputeUnits.cpuAndNeuralEngine`.
- Never silently fall back to CPU-only or GPU-only; raise a logged warning if
  ANE is unavailable at runtime.
- Use `coremltools >= 7.0` APIs. Do not use deprecated `MLModel` load paths.
- When writing CoreML conversion code, always set `compute_precision` to
  `ct.precision.FLOAT16` to maximise ANE throughput.

### 2. Zstandard Multi-Threading
- All zstd operations must pass `threads` (Python binding) or `--threads=0`
  (CLI) to saturate M-series Performance cores.
- Use dictionary-based compression (`ZstdCompressionDict`) when compressing
  sets of similar files (e.g., video segments, subtitle tracks).
- Minimum compression level: `zstd.COMPRESSION_LEVEL_DEFAULT` (3).
  Maximum for archival: level 19. Never use level > 19 in interactive paths.

## Code Style
- Python ≥ 3.12; use `match/case`, `tomllib`, `asyncio.TaskGroup` where natural.
- Prefer `pathlib.Path` over `os.path` everywhere.
- Use `rich` for all terminal output — no bare `print()` in production paths.
- Type-annotate all public functions with PEP 695 syntax where beneficial.

## Architecture Rules
- Entry point: `neural_rip.py` (root). Submodules in `scripts/`.
- CoreML models live in `models/` as `.mlpackage` bundles — never inline weights.
- No blocking I/O on the main thread; use `asyncio` or `concurrent.futures`.
- Secrets/tokens go in `.env` (python-dotenv); never hard-code or commit them.

## What NOT To Do
- Do not suggest GPU Metal shaders as a replacement for ANE paths.
- Do not add synchronous `time.sleep()` polling loops.
- Do not use `pickle` for model serialization — use CoreML `.mlpackage` only.
- Do not introduce dependencies that lack Apple Silicon wheels on PyPI.
